    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d111">

                <pb facs="0212" n="114" xml:id="pg114"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">574.D1/21: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wallace</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-03-17T17:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 17,
                                1920—5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>547. Following received from American Ambassador, London: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“My 414 of March 8, 5 p.m.<anchor n="86" xml:id="d111-f1"/>
                            Representative of the League of Nations informs me that conversations
                            regarding the conference are now taking place between the British and
                            French authorities and that the latter are demurring on the ground that
                            the question of the disposal of the German cable lines should not be
                            settled or influenced by non-members of the league.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>There is no intention to do this. Preliminary conference will discuss the agenda
                    for World Conference. German lines will be disposed of under the treaty and in
                    accordance with text of the agreement entered into in Paris which leaves them to
                    the disposition of Principal Allied and Associated Powers.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
